      "text": "{\"relation\": \"DIVERGENT\", \"consensus_summary\": \"The blind consensus identifies 'the truth' primarily as Jesus Christ himself, the personal embodiment of divine revelation (John 14:6), with closely allied referents: God's word/the gospel message, the Spirit of truth, sound doctrine, God's covenant faithfulness, and moral integrity. The Christological-personal identification is the settled center across all four responses.\", \"book_summary\": \"The book identifies 'the truth' as God's law — the standard. It explicitly flags the common teaching (Christ Himself, and God's word/gospel that sets free) as the view it departs from, citing Ps 119:142 ('thy law is the truth') and 2 John 4.\", \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Christ himself as the personal embodiment of divine truth, extended to God's word and the gospel that reveals him.\", \"book_core_identification\": \"God's law as the standard — 'the truth' is the divine law, not the person of Christ or the gospel message.\", \"core_relation\": \"CONTRADICTS\", \"extension_relation\": \"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\", \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book excludes the consensus's central cases — Christ in person (John 14:6) and the gospel message (Eph 1:13; Col 1:5) — while retaining only the Old Testament word/law strand (Ps 119:142) that the consensus treats as a secondary alternative or background. It effectively reclassifies Johannine 'truth that sets free' from gospel to law.\", \"material_contradiction\": \"The consensus core is a person (Christ) and the liberating gospel; the book's core is the law as standard. The book's own common-view note contrasts its identification against 'Christ Himself, and God's word — the gospel that sets free,' signaling deliberate replacement rather than refinement. In classical theology law and gospel are distinct categories, so identifying 'the truth' as the law-standard rather than the person of Christ or the gospel cannot both be the same core identification 